Restore a user at a restarted S-CSCF: assign_server() and Restoration-Info.

The only SAR the script could send for a request other than REGISTER was
assign_server_unreg(), which is UNREGISTERED_USER: it downloads the profile and
tells the HSS the user is unregistered at this S-CSCF. That is the right thing
for a terminating request to an unregistered user, and the wrong one for the
case TS 23.380 4.4.2 is about -- an S-CSCF that restarted and lost its usrloc
receiving an originating request from a UE that is still registered. There the
S-CSCF is to fetch the profile with NO_ASSIGNMENT, which changes nothing at the
HSS and which the HSS answers only for the S-CSCF already assigned to the user
(DIAMETER_UNABLE_TO_COMPLY otherwise, TS 29.228 6.1.2.1), and continue the
request as normal. There was no way to send it.

assign_server(async_route, domain, direction, type) is assign_server_unreg()
with the type as a fourth parameter, and assign_server_unreg() is now that with
UNREGISTERED_USER. Two types are accepted:

  0  NO_ASSIGNMENT      the profile is stored; an IMPU already in usrloc keeps
                        its registration state and bindings, a new one is
                        created IMPU_UNREGISTERED -- a profile and no binding,
                        which is what the usrloc timer makes of any record
                        without a valid contact anyway.
  3  UNREGISTERED_USER  as before.

REGISTRATION and RE_REGISTRATION belong to save(). The de-registration types
would have the HSS forget an assignment while usrloc keeps its bindings, since
nothing here tears them down, so they are refused.

The SAA callback decided whether to send the REGISTER's 200 and whether usrloc
was already updated by comparing the type with UNREGISTERED_USER. That is now a
flag on the saved transaction, set by assign_server() -- NO_ASSIGNMENT is the
type save() itself starts from before it picks the real one, so the type is not
a safe marker for "sent from the script".

Restoring the profile alone leaves a terminating request with no binding to
route to. The other half of 23.380 -- the S-CSCF backing up Path and Contact
at the HSS and restoring bindings from them -- is matched to open5gs
patches/0002 (images/open5gs), which backs up and returns TS 23.380 4.6.2
restoration information (an SCSCF-Restoration-Info AVP, TS 29.229 6.3.46)
alongside the profile on the same two SARs assign_server() sends:
NO_ASSIGNMENT for an S-CSCF restoring an originating user, and
UNREGISTERED_USER for a terminating request to one it has no data for.

Without a live binding, a request to that identity keeps getting 480 until
the UE's own registration timer fires -- correct per spec, but avoidable:
TS 23.380 4.3.2 describes the terminating case as "delivered, UE action:
none" precisely because the HSS can hand the S-CSCF a Contact and Path to
route through. Nothing before this sent the S-CSCF's own Path/Contact to
the HSS in the first place, and nothing read them back out of a restoring
SAA either.

cxdx_avp.c/.h: cxdx_add_scscf_restoration_info() and
cxdx_get_scscf_restoration_info(), the same shape as the existing
SIP-Auth-Data-Item builder and the Charging-Information getter. The AVP
codes (639/640/641/649, TS 29.229 6.3.46-48/52) were already in
cdp/diameter_ims_code_avp.h; only the two helper functions were missing.
The encoding is the two-level one 29.229 defines, with every member it
marks mandatory:

  SCSCF-Restoration-Info ::= < AVP Header: 639, 10415 >
                             { User-Name }
                           1*{ Restoration-Info }
  Restoration-Info       ::= < AVP Header: 649, 10415 >
                             { Path }
                             { Contact }

so the builder adds nothing unless it has all three of the private identity,
a Path and a Contact, and the getter looks for Path/Contact inside the first
Restoration-Info of the first SCSCF-Restoration-Info (an SAA may carry one
per private identity). 639/640/641/649 go out with only the V bit set: table
6.3.1 has them "M: must not".

cxdx_sar.c: cxdx_send_sar() attaches SCSCF-Restoration-Info -- the SAR's own
User-Name (the private identity), the REGISTER's own Path (path.c's
build_path_vector(), same as save()'s own usrloc write) and first Contact --
when the assignment type is REGISTRATION or
RE_REGISTRATION, i.e. only when msg actually is that REGISTER; for every
other assignment_type msg is the request being served, whose Path/Contact
have nothing to do with the served user's binding. With path_enabled off
there is no Path to back up, so no SCSCF-Restoration-Info goes out at all
rather than one missing a mandatory member. async_cdp_callback() reads
SCSCF-Restoration-Info back out of the SAA and passes it into update_contacts().

cxdx_avp.c/.h, cxdx_sar.c: every SAR also advertises the feature this is,
IMSRestorationInd -- bit 2 of Cx Feature-List-ID 1 (TS 29.229 table 7.1.1;
cdp already had the bit as AVP_IMS_Feature_List_ID_IMS_Restoration_Indication)
-- in a Supported-Features AVP (6.3.29: Vendor-Id 3GPP, Feature-List-ID 1,
Feature-List). The table has the HSS send restoration information only to an
S-CSCF that supports the feature, and 7.2 has a request list every feature it
was constructed with. The M bit follows 7.2.1: set on a (RE_)REGISTRATION SAR
that actually carries SCSCF-Restoration-Info, since that SAR uses the
feature; clear on every other SAR, NO_ASSIGNMENT and UNREGISTERED_USER
included, which use no feature themselves but are prepared for an SAA that
does. A consequence of the M bit, and the one the spec intends: an HSS
without the feature may refuse such a REGISTRATION SAR with
DIAMETER_ERROR_FEATURE_UNSUPPORTED (or DIAMETER_AVP_UNSUPPORTED if it has no
Supported-Features at all), which fails the REGISTER -- there is no re-send
with the feature dropped. The SAA's own Supported-Features is not read.

save.c: a new restore_ucontact(), called from update_contacts_sar_unregistered_user()
and update_contacts_sar_no_assignment() (both above) once their
own update_impurecord() succeeds. It inserts (or refreshes) a usrloc contact
the same way a real REGISTER would -- ul.get_ucontact()/insert_ucontact()/
update_ucontact(), the exact sequence update_contacts_helper() already uses --
except Call-ID and CSeq, which 23.380 4.6.2 lists as optional to back up and
this patch does not: a fixed low placeholder for each, so the UE's own next
REGISTER always supersedes them, and the module's own default_expires for
the expiry that was never backed up either. For NO_ASSIGNMENT this only fires
when the IMPU had no live binding already, so an originating restart-restore
never touches a binding the S-CSCF never actually lost (TS 23.380 4.4.2).

This alone does not change behaviour: nothing routes on the restored contact
without the matching serving.cfg change (route[TERM_RESTORE_SAA] now retries
lookup() after a restoring SAA before falling back to unregistered-service
handling), which is not a vendor patch and ships directly in cscf/.

Not carried, since 23.380 4.6.2 lists it as optional: the true registration
expiry, Call-ID, CSeq, or the 4.6.3/4.6.4 reg-event Subscription-Info used
for the post-delivery NOTIFY nudge. An HSS that never returns
SCSCF-Restoration-Info -- one predating open5gs patches/0002, or any other
vendor's -- leaves this patch a no-op: cxdx_get_scscf_restoration_info()
finds nothing and restore_ucontact() is never called with a contact to add.

ims_registrar_scscf_mod.c/.h, cxdx_sar.c: the whole of the above -- both the
SCSCF-Restoration-Info AVP and the Supported-Features AVP that advertises
IMSRestorationInd -- is behind a new modparam, scscf_restoration_info_enabled
(default 0). This is not about the read side, which is already a no-op
against a non-supporting HSS; it is about the mandatory Supported-Features
AVP itself, which every SAR carries once this is on. Per the M bit logic
above, a REGISTRATION/RE_REGISTRATION SAR that actually carries
SCSCF-Restoration-Info sets the M bit on its own Supported-Features AVP, and
an HSS without IMSRestorationInd must then reject that SAR outright
(DIAMETER_ERROR_FEATURE_UNSUPPORTED, or DIAMETER_AVP_UNSUPPORTED if it has no
Supported-Features support at all) -- failing the REGISTER, with no retry
that drops the feature. Off by default so an HSS that has not been confirmed
to support the feature is unaffected; an operator turns it on only once the
HSS is known to support it.

cxdx_sar.c, save.c: the terminating restoration answer is not a Result-Code
of DIAMETER_SUCCESS. TS 29.228 6.1.2.1 has the HSS answer an UNREGISTERED_USER
SAR for an identity it holds as registered, when IMS restoration is supported,
with all the S-CSCF Restoration Information and Experimental-Result-Code
DIAMETER_ERROR_IN_ASSIGNMENT_TYPE; TS 23.380 4.3.2 adds the user profile and
has the S-CSCF "trigger matched registered services". async_cdp_callback()
treated any SAA without a Result-Code as a Diameter error, so that answer
failed the request with 480 despite carrying everything needed to deliver
it. It now proceeds as on success when an UNREGISTERED_USER SAA has
DIAMETER_ERROR_IN_ASSIGNMENT_TYPE together with SCSCF-Restoration-Info; the
same code without restoration information is still an error. And
update_contacts_sar_unregistered_user() stores the IMPU as registered rather
than unregistered whenever the SAA carried restoration information, which it
does only for an identity the HSS holds as registered -- whichever result
code came with it, since open5gs patches/0002 answers the same case with
DIAMETER_SUCCESS.

diff --git a/src/modules/ims_registrar_scscf/cxdx_avp.c b/src/modules/ims_registrar_scscf/cxdx_avp.c
index a01e13e..d61fe85 100644
--- a/src/modules/ims_registrar_scscf/cxdx_avp.c
+++ b/src/modules/ims_registrar_scscf/cxdx_avp.c
@@ -534,6 +534,81 @@ int cxdx_add_server_name(AAAMessage *msg, str data)
 			IMS_vendor_id_3GPP, AVP_DUPLICATE_DATA, __FUNCTION__);
 }
 
+int cxdx_add_scscf_restoration_info(
+		AAAMessage *msg, str user_name, str path, str contact)
+{
+	AAA_AVP_LIST list, ri_list;
+	str group, ri_group;
+	list.head = 0;
+	list.tail = 0;
+	ri_list.head = 0;
+	ri_list.tail = 0;
+
+	/* User-Name, Path and Contact are all mandatory (TS 29.229 6.3.46,
+	 * 6.3.52): without one of them there is nothing valid to back up. */
+	if(!user_name.len || !path.len || !contact.len)
+		return 1;
+
+	/* Restoration-Info ::= { Path } { Contact } ...
+	 * 639/640/641/649 are V, M must not (TS 29.229 table 6.3.1) */
+	cxdx_add_avp_list(&ri_list, path.s, path.len, AVP_IMS_Path, 0,
+			IMS_vendor_id_3GPP, AVP_DONT_FREE_DATA, __FUNCTION__);
+	cxdx_add_avp_list(&ri_list, contact.s, contact.len, AVP_IMS_Contact, 0,
+			IMS_vendor_id_3GPP, AVP_DONT_FREE_DATA, __FUNCTION__);
+
+	ri_group = cdpb.AAAGroupAVPS(ri_list);
+
+	cdpb.AAAFreeAVPList(&ri_list);
+
+	/* SCSCF-Restoration-Info ::= { User-Name } 1*{ Restoration-Info } ... */
+	cxdx_add_avp_list(&list, user_name.s, user_name.len, AVP_User_Name,
+			AAA_AVP_FLAG_MANDATORY, 0, AVP_DONT_FREE_DATA, __FUNCTION__);
+	cxdx_add_avp_list(&list, ri_group.s, ri_group.len,
+			AVP_IMS_Restoration_Info, 0, IMS_vendor_id_3GPP, AVP_FREE_DATA,
+			__FUNCTION__);
+
+	group = cdpb.AAAGroupAVPS(list);
+
+	cdpb.AAAFreeAVPList(&list);
+
+	return cxdx_add_avp(msg, group.s, group.len,
+			AVP_IMS_SCSCF_Restoration_Info, 0, IMS_vendor_id_3GPP,
+			AVP_FREE_DATA, __FUNCTION__);
+}
+
+int cxdx_add_supported_features(
+		AAAMessage *msg, unsigned int feature_list, int mandatory)
+{
+	AAA_AVP_LIST list;
+	str group;
+	char x[4];
+	list.head = 0;
+	list.tail = 0;
+
+	/* Supported-Features ::= { Vendor-Id } { Feature-List-ID }
+	 * { Feature-List } (TS 29.229 6.3.29), Feature-List-ID 1 being the Cx
+	 * feature list of table 7.1.1 */
+	set_4bytes(x, IMS_vendor_id_3GPP);
+	cxdx_add_avp_list(&list, x, 4, AVP_Vendor_Id, AAA_AVP_FLAG_MANDATORY, 0,
+			AVP_DUPLICATE_DATA, __FUNCTION__);
+	set_4bytes(x, 1);
+	cxdx_add_avp_list(&list, x, 4, AVP_IMS_Feature_List_ID, 0,
+			IMS_vendor_id_3GPP, AVP_DUPLICATE_DATA, __FUNCTION__);
+	set_4bytes(x, feature_list);
+	cxdx_add_avp_list(&list, x, 4, AVP_IMS_Feature_List, 0,
+			IMS_vendor_id_3GPP, AVP_DUPLICATE_DATA, __FUNCTION__);
+
+	group = cdpb.AAAGroupAVPS(list);
+
+	cdpb.AAAFreeAVPList(&list);
+
+	/* 7.2.1: M set in a request constructed using one of the features,
+	 * clear in one only prepared to accept an answer that uses them */
+	return cxdx_add_avp(msg, group.s, group.len, AVP_IMS_Supported_Features,
+			mandatory ? AAA_AVP_FLAG_MANDATORY : 0, IMS_vendor_id_3GPP,
+			AVP_FREE_DATA, __FUNCTION__);
+}
+
 /**
  * Returns the SIP-Number-Auth-Items AVP from a Diameter message.
  * @param msg - the Diameter message
@@ -915,6 +990,52 @@ int cxdx_get_charging_info(
 	return 1;
 }
 
+int cxdx_get_scscf_restoration_info(AAAMessage *msg, str *path, str *contact)
+{
+	AAA_AVP_LIST list, ri_list;
+	AAA_AVP *avp;
+	str grp;
+
+	path->s = 0;
+	path->len = 0;
+	contact->s = 0;
+	contact->len = 0;
+
+	/* SCSCF-Restoration-Info ::= { User-Name } 1*{ Restoration-Info } ...
+	 * Restoration-Info ::= { Path } { Contact } ...  (TS 29.229 6.3.46/52)
+	 * An SAA may carry one per private identity, and each may carry more
+	 * than one Restoration-Info; the first of each is the one restored. */
+	grp = cxdx_get_avp(
+			msg, AVP_IMS_SCSCF_Restoration_Info, IMS_vendor_id_3GPP,
+			__FUNCTION__);
+	if(!grp.s)
+		return 0;
+
+	list = cdpb.AAAUngroupAVPS(grp);
+	avp = cdpb.AAAFindMatchingAVPList(
+			list, 0, AVP_IMS_Restoration_Info, IMS_vendor_id_3GPP, 0);
+	if(!avp) {
+		LM_WARN("SCSCF-Restoration-Info without Restoration-Info\n");
+		cdpb.AAAFreeAVPList(&list);
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	ri_list = cdpb.AAAUngroupAVPS(avp->data);
+	avp = cdpb.AAAFindMatchingAVPList(
+			ri_list, 0, AVP_IMS_Path, IMS_vendor_id_3GPP, 0);
+	if(avp)
+		*path = avp->data;
+
+	avp = cdpb.AAAFindMatchingAVPList(
+			ri_list, 0, AVP_IMS_Contact, IMS_vendor_id_3GPP, 0);
+	if(avp)
+		*contact = avp->data;
+
+	cdpb.AAAFreeAVPList(&ri_list);
+	cdpb.AAAFreeAVPList(&list);
+	return 1;
+}
+
